export const optimizingNextjsPerformance = "## Introduction\n\n**Fast dashboards win users, and Next.js gives you the primitives to hit great Core Web Vitals without hand-rolling a rendering framework.** Image-heavy admin dashboards are the hardest case: dozens of thumbnails, charts, and interactive widgets all competing for the main thread and the network. This article walks through a concrete performance playbook for the App Router (Next 14/15): what each Core Web Vital actually measures, how Server Components and streaming cut client JavaScript, how Partial Prerendering combines static and dynamic on one page, and how `next/image`, `next/font`, and lazy hydration keep your layout stable and your interactions snappy.\n\n## The Core Web Vitals, decoded\n\nThree field metrics define a \"good\" experience. INP replaced FID as a Core Web Vital in March 2024, so if your mental model still says \"First Input Delay,\" update it.\n\n| Metric | What it measures | \"Good\" threshold | Main lever |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| LCP | Time to render the largest element (hero image, big text block) | ≤ 2.5s | Image priority, server render, caching |\n| INP | Latency of interactions across the whole page visit | ≤ 200ms | Less client JS, lazy hydration, breaking up long tasks |\n| CLS | Unexpected layout movement | ≤ 0.1 | Sized images, font fallbacks, reserved space |\n\nThe trap on dashboards is that all three fight the same resource: the main thread. A large hydration bundle blocks interaction (INP), delays paint (LCP), and shifts content as widgets mount (CLS). The single most effective move is shipping less JavaScript to the client.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>Optimize with field data, not just lab data. Lighthouse is a synthetic snapshot; INP in particular only shows its true value under real user interaction. Pull Chrome UX Report data via <code>PageSpeed Insights</code> or the <code>web-vitals</code> library.</p></div></div>\n\n## Server Components: cut the JavaScript at the source\n\nIn the App Router, components are Server Components by default. They run only on the server, render to HTML, and ship **zero** JavaScript for their own logic. You opt into the client with `\"use client\"` — and everything imported by a Client Component becomes part of the client bundle.\n\nThe discipline: keep `\"use client\"` at the leaves of your tree, not the root. Fetch data and compose layout on the server; hand interactive islands the data they need as props.\n\n```tsx\n// app/dashboard/page.tsx — Server Component (no \"use client\")\nimport { getMetrics } from \"@/lib/metrics\";\nimport { RevenueChart } from \"./revenue-chart\"; // client island\nimport { StatCard } from \"./stat-card\";         // server component\n\nexport default async function DashboardPage() {\n  const metrics = await getMetrics(); // runs on the server, ships no fetch code\n\n  return (\n    <section className=\"grid gap-4\">\n      {metrics.summary.map((s) => (\n        <StatCard key={s.id} label={s.label} value={s.value} />\n      ))}\n      {/* Only the chart's JS reaches the browser */}\n      <RevenueChart series={metrics.series} />\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nBecause `getMetrics()` runs server-side, its dependencies (a database client, a date library, formatting helpers) never enter the client bundle. Only `RevenueChart` and its charting library are shipped.\n\n## Streaming with Suspense and loading.tsx\n\nDon't make the whole page wait for the slowest query. Wrap slow segments in `<Suspense>` and the server streams the shell immediately, then flushes each boundary as its data resolves.\n\n```tsx\n// app/dashboard/page.tsx\nimport { Suspense } from \"react\";\nimport { StatsSkeleton, TableSkeleton } from \"./skeletons\";\n\nexport default function DashboardPage() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <Suspense fallback={<StatsSkeleton />}>\n        <Stats />        {/* fast query */}\n      </Suspense>\n      <Suspense fallback={<TableSkeleton />}>\n        <RecentOrders /> {/* slow query, streams in later */}\n      </Suspense>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nA `loading.tsx` file is sugar for wrapping the whole route in a Suspense boundary — great for an instant route-level skeleton, while nested `<Suspense>` gives per-widget granularity.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Request] --> B[Server sends HTML shell]\n    B --> C[Fast Suspense boundary resolves]\n    C --> D[Slow boundary streams later]\n    D --> E[Hydrate client islands]\n    E --> F[Page fully interactive]\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Streaming improves perceived LCP because the browser paints the shell and skeleton right away. Put your <code>priority</code> hero image outside a Suspense boundary so it starts loading with the initial HTML.</p></div></div>\n\n## Partial Prerendering: static shell, dynamic holes\n\nPartial Prerendering (PPR) is the App Router feature that unifies static and dynamic rendering on a single route. At build time, Next prerenders a static shell. Anything wrapped in `<Suspense>` that reads dynamic data (cookies, `searchParams`, uncached fetches) becomes a \"hole\" streamed in at request time. Your CDN serves the shell instantly; the dynamic parts fill in.\n\n```tsx\n// next.config.ts\nimport type { NextConfig } from \"next\";\n\nconst config: NextConfig = {\n  experimental: {\n    ppr: \"incremental\", // opt in per route\n  },\n};\n\nexport default config;\n```\n\n```tsx\n// app/dashboard/page.tsx\nexport const experimental_ppr = true;\n\nexport default function Page() {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <StaticHeader />                 {/* prerendered into the static shell */}\n      <Suspense fallback={<Skeleton />}>\n        <PersonalizedFeed />           {/* dynamic hole, streamed per request */}\n      </Suspense>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>PPR is still experimental and requires the canary channel in Next 14/15. Don't ship it to production on a stable release expecting the exact same behavior — verify against the version you deploy, and gate it per route with <code>experimental_ppr</code>.</p></div></div>\n\n## next/image and next/font: kill LCP and CLS\n\nFor an image-heavy dashboard, `next/image` is where LCP is won or lost. It serves modern formats (AVIF/WebP), generates responsive `srcset`, and — crucially — reserves space to prevent layout shift.\n\n```tsx\nimport Image from \"next/image\";\n\n// The above-the-fold hero: mark it priority so it isn't lazy-loaded\n<Image\n  src=\"/hero.jpg\"\n  alt=\"Revenue overview\"\n  width={1200}\n  height={600}\n  priority                    // preloads; removes LCP from the lazy queue\n  sizes=\"100vw\"\n/>\n\n// A grid thumbnail: let sizes tell the browser the real rendered width\n<Image\n  src={item.thumb}\n  alt={item.name}\n  fill\n  sizes=\"(max-width: 768px) 50vw, 200px\"\n  className=\"object-cover\"\n/>\n```\n\nTwo rules that matter most: set `priority` on the single LCP image only (not everything — that defeats prioritization), and always set an accurate `sizes` so the browser downloads a 200px image for a 200px slot instead of the full-width source.\n\nFonts cause CLS when a fallback swaps to the web font at a different size. `next/font` self-hosts the font, inlines the CSS, and computes a matched fallback to keep layout stable.\n\n```tsx\n// app/layout.tsx\nimport { Inter } from \"next/font/google\";\n\nconst inter = Inter({\n  subsets: [\"latin\"],\n  display: \"swap\",\n  variable: \"--font-inter\",\n});\n\nexport default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  return (\n    <html lang=\"en\" className={inter.variable}>\n      <body>{children}</body>\n    </html>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Add a <code>blurDataURL</code> (or use <code>placeholder=\"blur\"</code> for static imports) on grid thumbnails. The blurry placeholder occupies the correct box immediately, so the swap to the real image costs zero CLS.</p></div></div>\n\n## Lazy hydration for heavy client widgets\n\nCharting libraries, rich text editors, and map components are heavy. If they're below the fold or behind a tab, don't hydrate them on load. `next/dynamic` code-splits and defers them; `ssr: false` skips server rendering entirely for browser-only widgets.\n\n```tsx\n\"use client\";\nimport dynamic from \"next/dynamic\";\n\n// The chart bundle loads only when this component renders\nconst RevenueChart = dynamic(() => import(\"./revenue-chart-impl\"), {\n  ssr: false,\n  loading: () => <div className=\"h-64 animate-pulse rounded bg-muted\" />,\n});\n\nexport function RevenueChart(props: { series: number[] }) {\n  return <RevenueChart {...props} />;\n}\n```\n\nThe payoff for INP: less JavaScript is parsed and executed on the main thread during initial hydration, so the page responds to clicks and typing sooner. Combine with `<Suspense>` and conditional rendering (only mount the widget when its tab is active) to keep long tasks off the critical path.\n\n## Caching, revalidation, and bundle analysis\n\nFast rendering means little if every request re-fetches. In the App Router, `fetch` is cached and deduplicated; control freshness explicitly.\n\n```ts\n// Time-based revalidation (ISR): re-generate at most every 60 seconds\nconst res = await fetch(\"https://api.example.com/metrics\", {\n  next: { revalidate: 60 },\n});\n\n// Per-route default\nexport const revalidate = 3600; // seconds\n\n// Opt out for always-fresh data\nconst live = await fetch(url, { cache: \"no-store\" });\n```\n\nUse `next: { tags: [\"metrics\"] }` with `revalidateTag(\"metrics\")` in a Server Action or route handler for precise, event-driven invalidation instead of blunt time windows.\n\nFinally, measure what you ship. `@next/bundle-analyzer` reveals which dependency bloated your client bundle — often a date or icon library imported into a Client Component.\n\n```js\n// next.config.js\nconst withBundleAnalyzer = require(\"@next/bundle-analyzer\")({\n  enabled: process.env.ANALYZE === \"true\",\n});\nmodule.exports = withBundleAnalyzer({});\n```\n\nRun `ANALYZE=true next build` and inspect the treemap.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-int\"><span class=\"ic\">🔥</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Interview question</b><p>\"Why did moving a component to a Server Component reduce the client bundle even though it imported a huge library?\" Because Server Component code and its imports never ship to the browser — only the serialized render output and any Client Components it references do.</p></div></div>\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n<div class=\"takeaways\"><ul>\n<li>LCP, INP, and CLS all compete for the main thread — shipping less client JavaScript improves all three at once.</li>\n<li>Keep <code>\"use client\"</code> at the leaves; fetch and compose on the server so heavy dependencies never reach the browser.</li>\n<li>Stream with <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> and <code>loading.tsx</code> to paint a shell instantly instead of blocking on the slowest query.</li>\n<li>Set <code>priority</code> on the one LCP image and accurate <code>sizes</code> on every other; use <code>next/font</code> to eliminate font-driven CLS.</li>\n<li>Defer heavy widgets with <code>next/dynamic</code> so hydration doesn't block the first interaction.</li>\n<li>Control freshness with <code>revalidate</code> and cache tags, and confirm your wins with a bundle analyzer, not guesses.</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Is INP the same as the old FID metric?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>No. First Input Delay only measured the delay before the first interaction was processed. INP (Interaction to Next Paint) measures latency across all interactions during the page visit and reports a near-worst-case value, so it's a much stricter reflection of real responsiveness. INP became a Core Web Vital in March 2024.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>When should I use loading.tsx versus a manual Suspense boundary?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Use <code>loading.tsx</code> for an instant route-level skeleton during navigation — it's the simplest win. Use explicit <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> boundaries inside the page when you want independent widgets to stream in at different times rather than the whole route waiting on one boundary.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Does ssr: false hurt SEO or LCP?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Only use <code>ssr: false</code> for genuinely client-only, non-critical UI — think below-the-fold charts or editors. For above-the-fold or SEO-relevant content, keep server rendering and defer hydration through streaming and code-splitting instead, so the HTML still contains the content.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Is Partial Prerendering production-ready?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>As of Next 14/15 it's experimental and typically requires the canary channel, enabled via <code>experimental.ppr</code> and opted into per route. It's powerful for pages that mix static and personalized content, but verify behavior on the exact version you deploy before relying on it in production.</p></div></details>\n";
